+
+**Amended 2026-09-29 (review round 2): reordering bounded, offset as a feasible interval.** Three changes to that extractor, each pinned by tests. (1) The retransmission proxy no longer counts "seq below the highest end seen": a bin keeps one bounded merged sequence-range set per directional 5-tuple (at most `SEQ_RANGES_CAP = 64` merged ranges; overflow is dropped and counted in `seq_range_sets_capped`) and counts a segment only when its **whole byte range was already observed in that bin** - so in-window reordering (seq 1000, 1020, then 1010) counts zero, keep-alives count zero, and RST and pure ACKs still count zero. (2) Reordering is no longer memory-unbounded: `reorder_grace_sec` defaults to 1 s (capture files are routinely written a little out of order) and is capped at `MAX_REORDER_GRACE_SEC = 300`, so an accumulator is retained for at most `bin_seconds + grace` of capture time, and `stats.peak_retained_bins` / `stats.watermark_scans` make both bounds visible. (3) `measure_clock_offset` no longer applies a median. Both clocks floor their timestamps, so one matched pair proves only that the true flow-minus-packet offset `theta` lies in `[delta - q_packet, delta + q_flow)`; the estimator intersects those per-pair constraints into the **feasible interval** `lo = max(delta) - q_packet`, `hi = min(delta) + q_flow`, *determines* each source's quantization step from its own values (the 0.001/1/60 s grids, >= 20 values, a declared step can never make the estimate more confident than the data allows), demotes the median to a dispersion statistic (it is biased low by up to one flow quantization step), and applies only the **midpoint of a non-empty interval at most `OFFSET_MAX_INTERVAL_WIDTH_SEC = 0.1 s` wide**, reporting `apply.max_error_sec` = half its width. An empty interval means no constant offset explains the pairs; a wide one means the offset is not pinned, and application is refused rather than guessed.
+
+**Amended 2026-09-29 (review round 3): late packets fail closed; the interval drops its outliers; the counter is named for what it counts.** (1) The interval is built from the **majority consensus window** only: the largest set of deltas a single constant offset could explain (those fitting one `q_flow + q_packet` window, found in O(n) with two pointers). Pairs outside it are counted in `observed_delta.n_inconsistent` and never averaged in, and if they exceed `OFFSET_MAX_INCONSISTENT_FRACTION = 5%` of all pairs that is a refusal in its own right (`flag: inconsistent_pairs`) - a DST jump, a mis-paired connection or a reused 5-tuple can no longer move the number that gets applied. (2) `on_backwards="merge"` is **gone**, replaced by `on_late={fail,drop}`: a packet whose bin already closed - one that arrived more than `bin_seconds + grace` after that bin's start - is *late*, and the default `on_late="fail"` aborts with `BackwardsTimestampError` naming the packet ts, the bin key it targets, the watermark and both remedies, while the CLI **exits 1 without writing any output file**, so a partial bin set can never present itself as a result. `on_late="drop"` is the only way to accept the loss, and it counts it in `pcap.backwards_packets_late_dropped` plus `pcap.bins_with_dropped_packets`, which names every `(src_ip, bin_start)` bin the packet would have filled. Reordering *inside* the grace needs no opt-in at all, because that bin is still open. Removing `merge` also removed the retained-accumulator state machine: every accumulator still in the map is open by definition and is freed the moment it closes, so nothing is ever kept alive for a packet that may not arrive. (3) The accumulator's counter is renamed `seq_regression_count` -> `dup_range_count`, named for what it counts; the emitted column stays `retransmission_count` because `PACKET_LEVEL_COLUMNS` freezes that name and `FEATURE_SEMANTICS` still carries the public wording. The dashboard demo path is a different, cruder estimator and now says so in both `features.py` and its own `approximations`: it is a **sequence-regression proxy (demo path only; counts reordered segments too)** - it remembers one high-water mark per direction, so seq 1000, 1020, 1010 scores 1 there and 0 through the streaming extractor, which a test pins by running the same capture both ways.
