+**Re-smoked 2026-09-29 (review round 3) on `data/sample_portscan.pcap`** - 200 directional 5-tuples in one 60 s bin - against two synthetic flow tables built from the capture itself, both planting the same +2.0 s flow-minus-packet offset. **Second-precision table** (the CIC `d/m/Y H:M:S` text format): measured n=200, all 200 consistent, delta span [+1.197, +1.249] s, `median(biased) = +1.220 s` - exactly the round-1/round-2 number, now labelled as the biased statistic it always was; `q_flow = 1 s`, `q_packet = 0.001 s`; feasible interval [+1.247972, +2.196924), **width 0.948952 s**: it contains the planted +2.0 s but sits ~9.5x over the 0.1 s bound, so `--apply-offset` **exits 1 and writes no output file** - second-precision flow timestamps cannot pin an offset to a tenth of a second, which is the honest answer and not a threshold to tune away. Without `--apply-offset` the same measurement is written with the refusal recorded in `clock_offset.apply`, coverage 1/1 bins and 1/1 attack bins. **Microsecond-precision table** (the same 200 rows with the sub-second kept): `q_flow = q_packet = 0.001 s`, delta [+2.000, +2.000], interval [+1.999000, +2.001000) - width 0.002 s - so its midpoint **is** applied: `+2.000000 s` with `max_error_sec = 0.0009998 s`, coverage again 1/1 and 1/1, all 8 packet columns populated. The late-packet policy is demonstrated on the same capture with one packet appended 7200 s early: the default run exits 1 naming `ts=1782470362.751028`, bin `10.0.4.12@1782470340`, `watermark=1782477562.803076` and both remedies, and writes nothing; `--on-late drop` exits 0 reporting 1 dropped packet against exactly that bin in `bins_with_dropped_packets`, 201 packets considered, `peak_retained_bins: 1`. `tests/test_pcap_bins.py` is now **64 tests**; the full suite is **119 passed, 4 skipped**. Clock offset and bin coverage **on real CIC PCAPs remain "not yet measured"** - the gates above are unchanged.
